{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices across most horizons (often +2 to +8 EUR). Apply a downward level shift to the entire path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Downtrend underestimation (lag): when recent history shows a clear decline, the forecast path decays too slowly and stays anchored near recent levels. Increase sensitivity to recent negative momentum (steepen the projected slope down).",
    "Mean-reversion/overshoot error: the model repeatedly projects rebounds back to ~70–74 after declines, but realized prices continue drifting lower. Reduce mean-reversion strength after a sustained selloff; require stabilization (e.g., 3–5 days flat/up) before forecasting a rebound.",
    "Horizon-dependent widening: error magnitude grows with lead time; short-term (1–5d) needs small adjustment, mid/long-term (10–30d) needs larger downward correction and less curvature upward.",
    "Volatility overprojection: forecast paths show higher oscillation and higher local peaks than realized. Damp day-to-day swings and cap local maxima relative to the starting level during bearish regimes."
  ]
}
